`if (name.len == strlen(entry_data[index].expected_name) && memcmp(name.value, entry_data[index].expected_name, name.len) == 0)`
is already safe and correct, as it checks for length equality before `memcmp` and `expected_name` has sufficient buffer size.

The fix involves restoring the original test logic for setting `entry_data[index].matched_name` within the `if (group == ...)` blocks, but replacing the unsafe `strcmp` calls with safe length-checked `memcmp` comparisons. This ensures that the buffer overflow is prevented while adhering to the test specification rule of not altering the test's expected behavior.

